+# Flu at Home: Household Guide
+
+## At a glance
+
+An adult housemate with flu should stay home and apart from others while ill. Normal activities can resume only after symptoms have been improving overall and there has been no fever for at least 24 hours without fever-reducing medicine. Because spreading the virus may still be possible, use added precautions for the following five days: cleaner air, good hygiene, distance, and a well-fitted mask around other people. If fever returns or the person feels worse, restart the stay-home period. [CDC respiratory-virus guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/respiratory-viruses/prevention/precautions-when-sick.html)
+
+## Reduce spread
+
+- Minimize close, face-to-face contact; when practical, have the sick adult use a separate resting space and keep distance from well housemates. [CDC caregiving guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/takingcare/caring-for-someone.html)
+- A well-fitted mask can add protection during necessary shared-room contact, especially when keeping distance is difficult. [CDC respiratory-virus guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/respiratory-viruses/prevention/precautions-when-sick.html)
+- Improve indoor air by bringing in fresh outdoor air, using exhaust fans, or filtering the air, while choosing options that are safe for the home. [CDC caregiving guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/takingcare/caring-for-someone.html)
+- Wash hands often after contact with the sick person, used tissues, or laundry, and have everyone cover each cough or sneeze with a tissue or elbow. [CDC caregiving guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/takingcare/caring-for-someone.html)
+- Regularly clean and disinfect frequently touched shared surfaces and objects, and avoid touching the eyes, nose, or mouth with unwashed hands. [CDC caregiving guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/takingcare/caring-for-someone.html)
+
+## When to get urgent help
+
+- Seek medical care right away for difficulty breathing, shortness of breath, or persistent pain or pressure in the chest or abdomen. [CDC flu warning signs](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/signs-symptoms/index.html)
+- Get urgent help for persistent dizziness, confusion, inability to arouse, or seizures. [CDC flu warning signs](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/signs-symptoms/index.html)
+- Treat not urinating, severe muscle pain, or severe weakness or unsteadiness as warning signs requiring prompt medical care. [CDC flu warning signs](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/signs-symptoms/index.html)
+- Contact a medical professional promptly if fever or cough improves but then returns or worsens, a chronic condition worsens, or any other symptom is severe or concerning. [CDC flu warning signs](https://www.cdc.gov/flu/signs-symptoms/index.html)
+
